    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3>Key Conclusions</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Structural regime changes dominate the narrative.</strong> Crude oil prices are not well-described by
      any single statistical model over the full period. Instead, the market has experienced distinct pricing regimes
      tied to cartel behavior, technological disruption, and geopolitical shocks. The CAGR of {cagr*100:.2f}% offers
      only a summary — the real story lies in the episodic booms and busts.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Volatility is a persistent feature, not a bug.</strong> With a standard deviation of ${price_std:.2f} —
      roughly {price_std/price_mean*100:.0f}% of the mean — oil prices are fundamentally volatile. Single-month moves
      exceeding ±10% have occurred in every decade except the early 1970s. Year-over-year moves exceeding ±40% have
      occurred five times.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The 2010s were the most expensive decade on record.</strong> At an average of ${decade_stats.loc[2010, 'Avg_Price']:.2f}/bbl,
      the 2010s surpassed even the 2000s commodity super-cycle, reflecting the high-price environment that persisted
      through mid-2014 before the shale-driven correction.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The energy transition introduces new uncertainty.</strong> The dataset through {last_date.strftime("%B %Y")} shows
      prices at ${last_price:.2f}. As the global economy navigates the tension between decarbonization goals and
      ongoing fossil fuel demand, oil price formation increasingly reflects both traditional supply-demand fundamentals
      and energy transition dynamics — including underinvestment in new supply and policy-driven demand shifts.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The range of plausible outcomes is wide.</strong> Historical precedent suggests that oil prices can
      double or halve within 12–18 months. The all-time range of ${price_min:.2f} to ${price_max:.2f} spans a
      factor of {price_max/price_min:.1f}x — a reminder that tail risks in both directions are a defining feature
