    <div class="value">${price_std:.2f}</div>
    <div class="detail">Std Dev of monthly prices</div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ── Section 1: Executive Overview ── -->
<div class="section">
  <h2>1. Executive Overview</h2>
  <p class="lead">
    This report analyzes <strong>{n_months} monthly observations</strong> of global crude oil prices spanning <strong>{date_range_str}</strong>.
    Over this {n_years:.1f}-year period, crude oil prices have undergone dramatic structural shifts — from the stable, low-price
    environment of the early 1970s (${first_price:.2f}/bbl) to the volatile, geopolitically-driven markets of today (${last_price:.2f}/bbl as of {last_date.strftime("%B %Y")}).
  </p>

  <div class="callout callout-insight">
    <strong>Key Finding:</strong> Crude oil prices have experienced a compound annual growth rate of <strong>{cagr*100:.2f}%</strong> over the full period.
    However, this headline figure masks extreme variability — the standard deviation of <strong>${price_std:.2f}</strong> exceeds
    the median price of <strong>${price_median:.2f}</strong>, underscoring the asset's inherent volatility.
  </div>

  <p>
    The dataset reveals five distinct price regimes: the pre-1973 stable era, the 1974–1985 OPEC-dominated high-price period,
    the 1986–2003 moderate-price era, the 2004–2014 commodity super-cycle, and the post-2014 shale-era rebalancing punctuated
    by the 2020 COVID-19 demand shock and the 2022 Russia-Ukraine price spike. Each of these regimes is characterized by
    fundamentally different supply-demand dynamics and geopolitical backdrops.
  </p>

  <h3>Key Statistics at a Glance</h3>
  <div class="stat-grid">
    <div class="stat-item"><span class="stat-label">All-Time Low</span><span class="stat-value">${price_min:.2f} ({df.loc[prices.idxmin(), "Date"].strftime("%b %Y")})</span></div>
    <div class="stat-item"><span class="stat-label">All-Time High</span><span class="stat-value">${price_max:.2f} ({df.loc[prices.idxmax(), "Date"].strftime("%b %Y")})</span></div>
    <div class="stat-item"><span class="stat-label">Mean Price</span><span class="stat-value">${price_mean:.2f}</span></div>
    <div class="stat-item"><span class="stat-label">Median Price</span><span class="stat-value">${price_median:.2f}</span></div>
    <div class="stat-item"><span class="stat-label">1-Year Max Increase</span><span class="stat-value">+{max_yoy_pct:.1f}% ({yoy_max_date.strftime("%b %Y")})</span></div>
    <div class="stat-item"><span class="stat-label">1-Year Max Decrease</span><span class="stat-value">{max_yoy_neg_pct:.1f}% ({yoy_min_date.strftime("%b %Y")})</span></div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ── Section 2: Long-Term Price Trajectory ── -->
<div class="section">
  <h2>2. Long-Term Price Trajectory (1970–2026)</h2>
  <p>
    The full time series reveals the epic scale of crude oil price movements across five-plus decades. Starting at just
    <strong>${first_price:.2f}/bbl</strong> in January 1970, prices remained below $3.00 through early 1973 before the
    OPEC oil embargo triggered a structural break. The overall trajectory shows a clear upward drift punctuated by
    episodic booms and busts.
  </p>

  <div class="chart-container">
    <img src="{chart_full_series}" alt="Full Historical Price Series">
    <div class="chart-caption">Figure 1: Monthly crude oil prices with major geopolitical and economic events annotated.</div>
  </div>

  <div class="callout callout-highlight">
    <strong>Structural Inflection Points:</strong> The chart highlights eight defining moments in oil market history.
    The 1973 embargo and 1979 Iranian Revolution established oil as a geopolitical weapon. The 1986 collapse reflected
    Saudi Arabia's decision to reclaim market share. The 2008 spike preceding the Global Financial Crisis marked the
    commodity super-cycle peak. COVID-19 drove the most sudden demand destruction in history, while the Russia-Ukraine
    conflict demonstrated that supply security concerns remain paramount.
  </div>

  <div class="chart-container">
    <img src="{chart_annual}" alt="Annual Average Price with Range">
    <div class="chart-caption">Figure 2: Annual average crude oil price with the monthly min-max range for each year.</div>
  </div>

  <p>
    The annual view emphasizes how intra-year volatility has intensified over time. Prior to 1974, the annual range was
    negligible. In 2008, prices swung from $89.52 to $132.83 within the year. In 2020, prices collapsed from $61.63 in
    January to $21.04 in April — a 66% drop in three months — before recovering to $48.73 by year-end.
  </p>

  <div class="chart-container">
    <img src="{chart_cumulative}" alt="Cumulative Price Growth">
    <div class="chart-caption">Figure 3: Cumulative growth multiplier since January 1970 (CAGR: {cagr*100:.2f}%).</div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ── Section 3: Decade-by-Decade Breakdown ── -->
<div class="section">
  <h2>3. Decade-by-Decade Breakdown</h2>
  <p>
    Grouping prices by decade reveals how profoundly the pricing environment has shifted across eras. The table below
